    "engine": "julia",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Boundaries\"\nengine: julia\n---\n\n\n\n\nNear the ends of the data, a kernel's stencil reaches beyond the last knot: `:b7` combines twelve\nvalues per evaluated point, so within six spacings of either end some of them do not exist.\nBoth packages fill those positions with ghost values extrapolated from the data, and the `bc`\nkeyword chooses how. The choice decides whether the interpolant keeps its full order up to the\nboundary, and what it does when the data near the boundary are not smooth.\n\n## The choices\n\n- `:poly` extrapolates with a polynomial of the degree the kernel reproduces. For smooth data\n  this keeps the full convergence order up to the boundary; it is the right choice whenever the\n  data continue smoothly past the last knot.\n- `:linear` and `:quadratic` extrapolate at lower order. They lose accuracy near the boundary\n  on smooth data, but they do not amplify a step or a kink there.\n- `:detect`, the default, uses `:poly` where the data near the boundary allow it and falls\n  back to `:linear` where they don't, decided separately at each boundary. It also falls back\n  when there are too few knots for the polynomial fit.\n\nIn Python the same options are strings: `\"poly\"`, `\"linear\"`, `\"quadratic\"`, `\"detect\"`.\n\n## When it matters\n\nSmooth data with a step close to the right end. Polynomial extrapolation continues the step's\nsteepness beyond the data and makes the interpolant overshoot between the last knots; `:detect`\nrecognises that the data there are not smooth and switches to linear extrapolation:\n\n::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## Julia\n\n::: {#4 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nxs = range(0, 1, length = 21)                             # 21 knots on [0, 1]\nys = sin.(3xs) .+ (xs .> 0.83)                            # smooth data with a step near the right end\nt = range(0.5, 1, length = 801)                           # fine points on the right half\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = \"x\", limits = ((0.5, 1), nothing))   # the right half only\nfor (bc, style) in ((:poly, :solid), (:linear, :solid), (:detect, :dash))\n    itp_bc = convolution_interpolation(xs, ys; bc = bc)   # the same data, three boundary conditions\n    lines!(ax, t, itp_bc.(t); linestyle = style, label = \"bc = :$bc\")\nend\nscatter!(ax, xs, ys; markersize = 10, color = :white, strokecolor = :black, strokewidth = 1.5, label = \"data\")\nLegend(fig[1, 2], ax)\nfig\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<img width=800 height=400 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n## Python\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom convinterp import convolution_interpolation\n\nxs = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0, 21)                            # 21 knots on [0, 1]\nys = np.sin(3.0 * xs) + np.where(xs > 0.83, 1.0, 0.0)     # smooth data with a step near the right end\nt = np.linspace(0.5, 1.0, 801)                            # fine points on the right half\n\nfor bc, style in [(\"poly\", \"-\"), (\"linear\", \"-\"), (\"detect\", \"--\")]:\n    itp_bc = convolution_interpolation(xs, ys, bc=bc)     # the same data, three boundary conditions\n    plt.plot(t, itp_bc(t), style, label=f'bc=\"{bc}\"')\nplt.plot(xs, ys, \"o\", mfc=\"white\", label=\"data\")          # the knots themselves\nplt.xlim(0.5, 1.0)\nplt.legend(loc=\"upper left\")\n```\n\n:::\n\nOn smooth data the picture reverses: the polynomial extrapolation is the accurate one, and the\nlinear fallback costs accuracy in the boundary cells. The same sine, interpolated with `:poly`\nand `:linear`, and the largest error measured in the two outermost cells on each side:\n\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nxs2 = range(0, 2π, length = 41)                           # 41 knots over one period\nys2 = sin.(xs2)                                           # smooth data\nh = step(xs2)                                             # the spacing\nedge = vcat(range(0, 2h, length = 201), range(2π - 2h, 2π, length = 201))   # the outermost two cells on each side\n\nprintln(\"| bc | largest error in the boundary cells |\")\nprintln(\"|:--|--:|\")\nfor bc in (:poly, :linear, :detect)\n    itp_bc = convolution_interpolation(xs2, ys2; bc = bc)\n    @printf(\"| `:%s` | %.2e |\\n\", bc, maximum(abs.(itp_bc.(edge) .- sin.(edge))))\nend\n```\n| bc | largest error in the boundary cells |\n|:--|--:|\n| `:poly` | 2.32e-09 |\n| `:linear` | 6.06e-05 |\n| `:detect` | 2.32e-09 |\n\n\n\n`:detect` matches `:poly` here, as it should: the data are smooth at both ends, so nothing\ntriggers the fallback.\n\n## Per side and per axis\n\nThe boundary condition can be given per side, as a `(left, right)` pair, and in more\ndimensions per axis, as a tuple of such pairs:\n\n::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## Julia\n\n```julia\nitp = convolution_interpolation(x, y; bc = (:poly, :linear))          # polynomial on the left, linear on the right\nitp = convolution_interpolation((x, y), z; bc = ((:poly, :poly),      # first axis\n                                                 (:detect, :linear))) # second axis\n```\n\n## Python\n\n```python\nitp = convolution_interpolation(x, y, bc=(\"poly\", \"linear\"))          # polynomial on the left, linear on the right\nitp = convolution_interpolation((x, y), z, bc=((\"poly\", \"poly\"),      # first axis\n                                               (\"detect\", \"linear\"))) # second axis\n```\n\n:::\n\n## Outside the data\n\nEverything above concerns points inside the data range; the ghost values exist so that the\nstencil is complete there. What happens for a point outside the range is a separate choice, the\n`extrap` keyword of the Julia package:\n\n- `:throw`, the default, raises an error — the safe choice, since nothing outside the data is\n  known.\n- `:flat` returns the value at the nearest boundary.\n- `:line` continues linearly from the boundary.\n- `:natural` extends the data by the kernel's own extrapolation and interpolates past the\n  boundary, so derivatives stay continuous across it. It extrapolates twice, which becomes\n  expensive in higher dimensions.\n\n```julia\nitp = convolution_interpolation(x, y; extrap = :natural)  # evaluate a little beyond the data\nitp(-0.1)                                                 # no error: extrapolated\n```\n\nThe Python port does not extrapolate yet: points outside the data range raise a `ValueError`.\nThe [feature matrix](../features.qmd) tracks this.\n\n",
